</section>

<section style="padding:4rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid #e00;">
  <div class="container">
    <div style="max-width:800px;">
      <p style="font-size:0.6rem;letter-spacing:0.2em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#e00;margin-bottom:0.75rem;">Styles &amp; Fits</p>
      <h2 style="font-size:1.4rem;font-weight:300;color:#fff;margin-bottom:1.5rem;">Hoodie Styles We Produce</h2>
      <p style="color:#FFFFFF;font-size:0.9rem;line-height:1.9;margin-bottom:1rem;"><strong style="color:#fff;font-weight:500;">Pullover</strong> with a kangaroo pocket is the volume style and our most refined pattern. <strong style="color:#fff;font-weight:500;">Full-zip</strong> needs a heavier-gauge zipper matched to fleece weight — a zipper too light for a 400gsm fleece will strain at the chin guard within a few washes, so we spec zipper gauge against your fabric choice before cutting. <strong style="color:#fff;font-weight:500;">Half-zip</strong> sits between the two structurally, closer to pullover construction with a shorter zip run at the collar.</p>
      <p style="color:#FFFFFF;font-size:0.9rem;line-height:1.9;">On fit, we grade <strong style="color:#fff;font-weight:500;">oversized and boxy</strong> streetwear blocks with dropped shoulders, <strong style="color:#fff;font-weight:500;">cropped</strong> lengths for fashion-forward lines, and <strong style="color:#fff;font-weight:500;">standard regular fit</strong> for general retail. Sleeve length and body length are the two dimensions clients most often want to adjust from a reference garment, and both are straightforward pattern changes once we have your spec sheet.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section style="padding:4rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid #e00;">
  <div class="container">
    <div style="max-width:800px;">
      <p style="font-size:0.6rem;letter-spacing:0.2em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#e00;margin-bottom:0.75rem;">Construction Guide</p>
      <h2 style="font-size:1.4rem;font-weight:300;color:#fff;margin-bottom:1.5rem;">Hoodie Fabric &amp; Construction Options</h2>
      <p style="color:#FFFFFF;font-size:0.9rem;line-height:1.9;margin-bottom:1rem;">Fabric choice drives both feel and cost. <strong style="color:#fff;font-weight:500;">Brushed fleece</strong> (240–320gsm) is the standard choice for everyday hoodies — soft inner face, warm, moderate cost. <strong style="color:#fff;font-weight:500;">French terry</strong> (260–340gsm) has a looped, uncut inner face — lighter, more breathable, and increasingly popular for premium streetwear. <strong style="color:#fff;font-weight:500;">Loopback fleece</strong> sits between the two, with a slightly finer loop structure and better shape retention after washing.</p>
      <p style="color:#FFFFFF;font-size:0.9rem;line-height:1.9;margin-bottom:1rem;">Hood construction affects both fit and cost. A lined hood (fabric folded and stitched double) holds its shape better and drapes more cleanly, but adds fabric and labor cost versus a single-layer hood. Drawcords can be flat cotton tape, round cord, or ropes with metal or plastic aglets — each needs its own eyelet reinforcement, which we spec in the tech pack review before sampling.</p>
      <p style="color:#FFFFFF;font-size:0.9rem;line-height:1.9;">Pocket style is a simple but visible choice: a <strong style="color:#fff;font-weight:500;">kangaroo pocket</strong> (single pouch, both hands enter from the sides) is the classic option and slightly cheaper to construct. A <strong style="color:#fff;font-weight:500;">split pocket</strong> (two separate compartments) costs a little more in stitching time but is preferred by some streetwear brands for its cleaner front panel line.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section style="padding:4rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid #e00;">
  <div class="container">
    <div style="max-width:800px;">
      <p style="font-size:0.6rem;letter-spacing:0.2em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#e00;margin-bottom:0.75rem;">Decoration</p>
      <h2 style="font-size:1.4rem;font-weight:300;color:#fff;margin-bottom:1.5rem;">Printing &amp; Embellishment on Fleece</h2>
      <p style="color:#FFFFFF;font-size:0.9rem;line-height:1.9;margin-bottom:1rem;"><strong style="color:#fff;font-weight:500;">Embroidery</strong> is in-house and the standard for chest, sleeve, and hood-panel logos — on lighter fleece we back it with a stabilizer to stop puckering, since a bare embroidery run on thin brushed fleece will pull and distort around dense stitch areas. <strong style="color:#fff;font-weight:500;">Puff print</strong> gives a raised, 3D texture that reads well at larger chest-logo sizes and works cleanly on fleece above 260gsm. See our <a href="/embroidery-service/" style="color:#fff;border-bottom:1px solid #e00;text-decoration:none;">embroidery service</a> page for digitizing and stitch-count detail.</p>
      <p style="color:#FFFFFF;font-size:0.9rem;line-height:1.9;">Screen print, DTF, and heat transfer are all available for graphic and photographic front/back designs. Screen print is the more economical choice at higher volumes with simple color counts; DTF suits smaller runs or gradient-heavy artwork. Our <a href="/insights/printing-methods-compared/" style="color:#fff;border-bottom:1px solid #e00;text-decoration:none;">printing methods comparison</a> breaks down cost and durability by technique if you're deciding between them.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section style="padding:4rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid #e00;">
  <div class="container">
    <div style="max-width:800px;">
      <p style="font-size:0.6rem;letter-spacing:0.2em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#e00;margin-bottom:0.75rem;">Order Types</p>
      <h2 style="font-size:1.4rem;font-weight:300;color:#fff;margin-bottom:1.5rem;">Private Label, OEM, and Blank Wholesale</h2>
      <p style="color:#FFFFFF;font-size:0.9rem;line-height:1.9;margin-bottom:1rem;">Most hoodie clients order <strong style="color:#fff;font-weight:500;">private label</strong> — your woven neck labels, care labels, and packaging built onto our production, sourced through our label suppliers or yours if you already have one. <strong style="color:#fff;font-weight:500;">OEM</strong> follows your tech pack exactly with no design input from us. If you're supplying your own fleece and only need cut-make-trim, that's a <strong style="color:#fff;font-weight:500;">CMT</strong> arrangement with a 5,000-piece minimum instead of the 300-piece OEM/ODM MOQ, since sourcing and dyeing a fleece lot for one client doesn't scale below that volume.</p>
      <p style="color:#FFFFFF;font-size:0.9rem;line-height:1.9;">Still deciding which model fits your brand? Our <a href="/insights/oem-vs-odm-vs-cmt/" style="color:#fff;border-bottom:1px solid #e00;text-decoration:none;">OEM vs ODM vs CMT comparison</a> and <a href="/white-label-manufacturer/" style="color:#fff;border-bottom:1px solid #e00;text-decoration:none;">private label vs white label guide</a> walk through the practical differences before you commit to a tech pack.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section style="padding:4rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid #e00;">
  <div class="container">
    <div style="max-width:800px;">
      <p style="font-size:0.6rem;letter-spacing:0.2em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#e00;margin-bottom:0.75rem;">Getting Started</p>
      <h2 style="font-size:1.4rem;font-weight:300;color:#fff;margin-bottom:1.5rem;">Sampling, Sizing &amp; Lead Time</h2>
      <p style="color:#FFFFFF;font-size:0.9rem;line-height:1.9;margin-bottom:1rem;">A proto sample typically takes 10-14 days once we have your tech pack, and up to 10 free revision rounds are included before bulk cutting starts. Heavier fleece and lined-hood constructions tend to push toward the longer end of that window since there's more to fit-check. Full size runs are graded against your spec sheet or, if you don't have one yet, our <a href="/insights/international-size-chart-guide/" style="color:#fff;border-bottom:1px solid #e00;text-decoration:none;">size chart guide</a> covering US, UK, and EU conversions. Bulk production runs 30-45 days after pre-production sample sign-off, with AQL 2.5 inspection before shipment.</p>
      <p style="color:#FFFFFF;font-size:0.9rem;line-height:1.9;">Labels and packaging — woven neck labels, care labels, hang tags, poly bags — get sourced and set up during sampling. Our <a href="/garment-labels/types-of-garment-labels/" style="color:#fff;border-bottom:1px solid #e00;text-decoration:none;">garment labels guide</a> covers the label types worth knowing before you brief your first order.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section style="padding:4rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid #e00;">
  <div class="container">
    <p style="font-size:0.6rem;letter-spacing:0.2em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#e00;margin-bottom:0.75rem;">Common Questions</p>
    <h2 style="font-size:1.4rem;font-weight:300;color:#fff;margin-bottom:1.5rem;">Hoodie Manufacturer FAQ</h2>
    <div style="max-width:800px;">
      <div style="border-bottom:1px solid #e00;padding:1.5rem 0;">
        <h3 style="font-size:1rem;font-weight:500;color:#fff;margin-bottom:0.75rem;">What's the minimum order for a hoodie style with multiple colors?</h3>
        <p style="color:#FFFFFF;font-size:0.88rem;line-height:1.8;">The 300-piece MOQ applies per style for OEM and ODM orders, and can typically be split across 3-4 colors and a full size range within that single style minimum — you don't need 300 pieces of one exact color. CMT orders where you supply your own fabric run at a 5,000-piece minimum instead. Need to start smaller? See our <a href="/small-batch-hoodie-manufacturer/" style="color:#fff;border-bottom:1px solid #e00;text-decoration:none;">small batch hoodie manufacturer</a> page.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="border-bottom:1px solid #e00;padding:1.5rem 0;">
        <h3 style="font-size:1rem;font-weight:500;color:#fff;margin-bottom:0.75rem;">Can you print or embroider on fleece without distorting the fabric?</h3>
        <p style="color:#FFFFFF;font-size:0.88rem;line-height:1.8;">Yes, with the right method for the fabric weight. Puff print and embroidery both work well on brushed fleece and French terry above 260gsm; on lighter fleece we recommend a stabilizer backing for embroidery to prevent puckering.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="border-bottom:1px solid #e00;padding:1.5rem 0;">
        <h3 style="font-size:1rem;font-weight:500;color:#fff;margin-bottom:0.75rem;">What's the difference between brushed fleece and French terry for my brand?</h3>
        <p style="color:#FFFFFF;font-size:0.88rem;line-height:1.8;">Brushed fleece is warmer and has a more traditional hoodie feel at a lower cost — good for basics and high-volume programs. French terry is lighter, drapes better, and reads as a more premium product — common for streetwear and elevated basics lines willing to pay a moderate premium per piece.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="border-bottom:1px solid #e00;padding:1.5rem 0;">
        <h3 style="font-size:1rem;font-weight:500;color:#fff;margin-bottom:0.75rem;">Do you offer oversized or cropped hoodie fits?</h3>
        <p style="color:#FFFFFF;font-size:0.88rem;line-height:1.8;">Yes. We grade to your spec sheet or reference garment — oversized, boxy, cropped, and standard fits are all available from the same fabric and construction options, provided the pattern is confirmed at the sampling stage.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="border-bottom:1px solid #e00;padding:1.5rem 0;">
        <h3 style="font-size:1rem;font-weight:500;color:#fff;margin-bottom:0.75rem;">Can you produce private label hoodies with my own brand labels?</h3>
        <p style="color:#FFFFFF;font-size:0.88rem;line-height:1.8;">Yes, private label is the default setup for most hoodie clients — your woven neck labels, care labels, and packaging on our production. We source labels directly or work from your existing supplier if you already have one.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="padding:1.5rem 0;">
        <h3 style="font-size:1rem;font-weight:500;color:#fff;margin-bottom:0.75rem;">How long does a hoodie order take from sample approval to shipment?</h3>
        <p style="color:#FFFFFF;font-size:0.88rem;line-height:1.8;">30-45 days for bulk production after you approve the pre-production sample, with heavier fleece and lined-hood constructions running toward the longer end. Proto and fit samples before that typically take 10-14 days, with up to 10 free revision rounds included. Our <a href="/clothing-manufacturing-cost-guide/" style="color:#fff;border-bottom:1px solid #e00;text-decoration:none;">manufacturing cost guide</a> covers what drives price per unit beyond fabric weight.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

